-- RECOGNITION — Pass 116 §0a, made executable. Reaffirmed by Pass 118 §0h.
--
-- The ruling: *"no `std.mem` / `std.fmt` / `std.math`. Pure ops are VALUE EDGES
-- (`x:abs()`, `s:copy()`); worlds are the ONLY capability namespaces. Idiom
-- recognition lowers to intrinsics UNDER WITNESS — `why(realization)` answers."*
-- §2 AUDIT v4 names a **recognition scan** as a row. This file is that row.
--
-- WHY IT IS A SEPARATE GATE AND NOT A ROW INSIDE audit100, stated rather than
-- quietly decided. audit100 ratchets one budget per denied SPELLING, and the
-- whole finding here is that one spelling is TWO different rules with two
-- different blockers and two different repairs. A single `math.` budget would
-- average them and hide exactly the thing worth seeing. audit100 is also at
-- budget on every row; a new row there buys a merge conflict, not a signal.
--
-- THE SPLIT, which is the point of the file. `math.` is one namespace wearing
-- two faces:
--
--   pure    abs floor sqrt min max pi cos sin pow log exp ceil …
--           NO capability. §0a says these are VALUE EDGES on the descriptor:
--           `x:abs()`, `(2.5):floor()`. Repair is a surface that does not
--           exist yet — see BLOCKER 1.
--
--           THE OBVIOUS SHORTCUT IS FORECLOSED, and it is worth writing down
--           because it is what a hurried migration would reach for: moving the
--           pure ops into some other namespace — `std.numeric`, `num.`, a
--           renamed `math` — is NOT a repair. Pass 118 §0h says std has NO
--           MODULE TREE and that WORLDS ARE THE ONLY NAMESPACES, so a fresh
--           namespace for capability-free ops is the same defect respelled,
--           and it would move this row to zero while changing nothing. The
--           only legal destination is the descriptor.
--   rand    math.random / math.randomseed
--           NOT pure. Ambient authority, and §0a says worlds are the ONLY
--           capability namespaces — which is also precisely what Pass 105 U1
--           (`std@{ ambient = false }`) demands. Repair is a world. See
--           BLOCKER 2.
--   sub     math.fft / math.stats / math.prng / math.geometry …
--           MODULE references, not ops. They ride the `math.` spelling but the
--           ruling is about op namespaces, so they are reported and not
--           ratcheted — a ratchet on them would be measuring a directory name.
--   ns      std.mem / std.fmt / std.math — the three the ruling names outright.
--
-- THE LEVERAGE, and it is the reason this file exists on the same night as the
-- capability scan: `rand` is not a side note. It is the ENTIRE `rand` class of
-- Pass 105 U1 — 50 sites across 14 modules, the third-largest ambient class in
-- `lib/std`. gaps/GAP-061.md reported `rand` at ZERO because it grepped for
-- `rand(`; this tree spells it `math.random(`. Retiring `math.random` and
-- closing U1's rand class are THE SAME EDIT. `zig build capability-scan` is the
-- instrument that proves it: every converted site must lower ITS number too.
--
-- WHAT GAP-071 MEASURED AND WHAT IT MISSED. GAP-071 records this ruling's
-- distance as `std.math. 16 files / std.mem. 1 / std.fmt. 1`. Those are the
-- DOTTED-NAMESPACE counts. The dominant spelling in this tree is BARE `math.`,
-- which GAP-071 never counted and which AUDIT v4 denies by name. It is 79
-- files. A deny row measured at 16 that is really 79 reads as nearly-satisfied
-- when it is not, which is the exact failure GAP-061 and GAP-071 were both
-- written to stop repeating.
--
-- THE TWO BLOCKERS, PROBED LIVE rather than asserted, because a blocker
-- recorded in prose is a blocker nobody notices lifting:
--
--   BLOCKER 1 — the value-edge surface DOES NOT EXIST. `x:abs()` on a scalar
--   does not compile. The compiler lowers the receiver away entirely and emits
--   a call to an undeclared `a__abs()` with ZERO arguments. That is worse than
--   a missing feature: it is a shape that would bind to any same-named free
--   function and silently drop the subject. `probecompile` below compiles the
--   target form on every run and reports whether it still fails.
--
--   BLOCKER 2 — there is no WORLD. `world` appears nowhere in `lib/std` except
--   one line of prose, and `docs/spec/grammar.md` (Pass 108, NORMATIVE) does
--   not contain the word at all. There is no construct to move `math.random`
--   behind. `grammar` below greps the normative grammar on every run.
--
--   Pass 118 §0h RAISES the cost of this blocker rather than lowering it:
--   worlds are now the only namespaces std has at all, so the construct that
--   `math.random` is waiting on is the same construct the whole prelude graph
--   is waiting on. This row cannot be closed by a local edit in `lib/std`, and
--   any commit claiming to have closed it should be read closely.
--
-- Both probes are PAIRED with a positive control, because "the target form
-- failed to compile" and "nothing compiles / the grep is broken" are the same
-- red otherwise. A probe that cannot distinguish those two is not a probe.
--
-- THE RATCHET. `pure`, `rand` and `ns` carry budgets; `sub` is reported only.
-- Budgets are EXACT — no headroom, for the reason capability_scan gives:
-- headroom is a licence to add one more, and one more is the only kind of
-- regression anyone introduces. Lowering a budget is the whole point; raising
-- one needs a sentence in the commit message.
--
-- Sites are counted in LINES, not occurrences, so a line calling `math.floor`
-- twice counts once. This is the same convention capability_scan uses.
--
-- TWO NUMBERS THAT LOOK WRONG AND ARE NOT, recorded so nobody re-derives them:
--
--   `rand` reads 51 here and 50 in capability_scan. The difference is
--   `lib/std/math.duo:200-201`, `M.random = math.random` and its `randomseed`
--   twin — RE-EXPORT ALIASES, not calls. capability_scan's pattern requires a
--   trailing `(` and cannot see them; its own header lists aliases as a known
--   blind spot. This row does not require the paren, because an alias that
--   hands `math.random` to every importer IS the ambient reach, and it is the
--   single site whose removal would delete the most authority in the tree.
--
--   `sub` reads 0, and a 0 on a fresh probe is usually a broken probe. It is
--   not one here: the detector fires on its own example under the control
--   below, and the submodules DO exist — they are spelled `std.math.fft`, so
--   they land on the `ns` row. There is no BARE `math.fft` in the tree. The
--   row stays because the spelling is one edit away from appearing.
--
-- POPULATION. Tracked `lib/std/**/*.duo` — the shipped standard library, the
-- thing the ruling is about. The rest of the tree is REPORTED so the number is
-- not mistaken for a whole-repo claim.
--
-- Exit 0 at or under budget · 1 over · 3 the scan could not run.
--
-- RECOGNITION_PURE_FLOOR / _RAND_FLOOR / _NS_FLOOR override the budgets.
-- RECOGNITION_SHOW=1 prints the per-module offender list.

q = "'"

comments = "(^|:)[0-9][0-9]*:[[:space:]]*--"

-- Every pattern requires the op to be preceded by something that is neither an
-- identifier character nor a dot, so `std.math.floor` and `mymath.floor` are
-- both declined by the bare rows. `std.math.` is the `ns` row's business, and a
-- site counted twice would make the two budgets move together for no reason.
purepat = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.:])math\\.(abs|floor|ceil|sqrt|min|max|pi|huge|cos|sin|tan|cosh|sinh|tanh|acos|asin|atan|pow|log|exp|ldexp|clamp|sign|modf|fmod|rad|deg|erf|maxinteger|mininteger|type)([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)"

randpat = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.:])math\\.(random|randomseed)([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)"

subpat = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.:])math\\.(fft|stats|prng|noise|cmplx|wavelet|interpolate|geometry|fast|bits|optimize)([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)"

nspat = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.:])std\\.(mem|fmt|math)([^A-Za-z0-9_]|$)"

space = " "

-- The shell prefix every count shares. No list file on purpose: a fixed path
-- under /tmp is a shared mutable surface and this repository has already had
-- one run's numbers printed under another run's banner because of it. `-H`
-- because a single-file xargs batch drops the path prefix and would take the
-- module census with it.
head(root: str): str
    "cd {root} && git ls-files 'lib/std/*.duo' | tr '\\n' '\\0' | xargs -0 grep -H -n -E "
end

-- Matching lines, whole-line comments dropped.
hits(root: str, pat: str): i64
    pre: str = head(root)
    cmd: str = "{pre}{q}{pat}{q} 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E {q}{comments}{q} | wc -l"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

-- Distinct modules holding at least one site of this class.
modules(root: str, pat: str): i64
    pre: str = head(root)
    cmd: str = "{pre}{q}{pat}{q} 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E {q}{comments}{q} | cut -d: -f1 | sort -u | wc -l"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

-- The same class over everything tracked that is NOT lib/std. Reported so the
-- lib/std budget is never read as a whole-repo claim.
--
-- THIS FILE EXCLUDES ITSELF, for the reason audit100 gives about its own
-- corpus: the patterns are DATA, and a scanner whose haystack contains its own
-- needles measures itself. Caught rather than reasoned: staging this file moved
-- `outside` from 50/4/42/0 to 56/6/48/1 with nothing in the tree having
-- changed — every one of those six new "sites" was a regex literal in the
-- lines above. The lib/std rows never had the problem (this file is not in
-- lib/std) but the exclusion is written against the pathspec, not the
-- directory, so moving the file cannot silently reintroduce it.
outside(root: str, pat: str): i64
    cmd: str = "cd {root} && git ls-files '*.duo' | grep -v -E {q}^lib/std/{q} | grep -v -E {q}^scripts/recognition{q} | tr '\\n' '\\0' | xargs -0 grep -H -n -E {q}{pat}{q} 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E {q}{comments}{q} | wc -l"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

-- One synthetic line through the ENTIRE filter chain.
probe(pat: str, line: str): i64
    cmd: str = "printf {q}%s\\n{q} {q}{line}{q} | grep -n -E {q}{pat}{q} | grep -v -E {q}{comments}{q} | wc -l"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

-- The same line with NO filter. `probe` alone cannot tell "the filter deleted
-- it" from "the pattern never matched it"; every filter check is a PAIR.
raw(pat: str, line: str): i64
    cmd: str = "printf {q}%s\\n{q} {q}{line}{q} | grep -c -E {q}{pat}{q}"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

pad(s: str, width: i64): str
    out = s
    while #out < width
        out = out .. space
    end
    out
end

row(name: str, sites: i64, mods: i64, rest: i64, note: str): void
    a = pad(name, 8)
    b = pad(sites:to(str), 7)
    c = pad(mods:to(str), 7)
    d = pad(rest:to(str), 9)
    print("  {a}{b}{c}{d}  {note}")
end

-- A detector must see its own example and decline ordinary Duo. Both halves,
-- or the row is either dead or noise.
sound(pat: str, example: str): bool
    good: i64 = probe(pat, example)
    bad: i64 = probe(pat, "  x = f(y) + 1")
    good == 1 and bad == 0
end

-- BLOCKER 1, live. Builds the target form §0a asks for and RUNS it, asserting
-- the VALUE: `(-3.0):abs()` must be 3.0. Returns 1 only if the program compiles
-- AND answers correctly. The caller pairs this with `probecontrol`; on its own
-- a 0 cannot tell a missing feature from a missing compiler.
--
-- `duo compile`, NEVER `duo check`. This is not a stylistic preference and it
-- cost a false green to learn: `duo check` EXITS 0 on `a:abs()`. It is the
-- CODEGEN that rejects it, and the first draft of this gate reported BLOCKER 1
-- as LIFTED because check answered green on a form that cannot be built. A
-- probe that stops before the stage that fails is not a probe.
--
-- And it runs the binary rather than trusting the build, because `x:abs()`
-- returning the WRONG SIGN would compile perfectly.
probecompile(root: str, bin: str, work: str): i64
    src: str = "{work}/probe.duo"
    body: str = "main(): i64\\n    a: f64 = -3.0\\n    r: f64 = a:abs()\\n    if r == 3.0 return 0 end\\n    1\\nend\\n"
    cmd: str = "cd {work} && printf {q}{body}{q} > {src} && {root}/{bin} compile {src} -o {work}/probe.out >/dev/null 2>&1 && {work}/probe.out >/dev/null 2>&1 && echo 1 || echo 0"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

-- The positive control for BLOCKER 1: the SAME pipeline, the same binary, the
-- same scratch directory, the same asserted VALUE — carrying the spelling that
-- works today. If this reads 0 the apparatus is broken and the blocker verdict
-- must be WITHHELD rather than reported as confirmed.
probecontrol(root: str, bin: str, work: str): i64
    src: str = "{work}/control.duo"
    body: str = "main(): i64\\n    a: f64 = -3.0\\n    r: f64 = math.abs(a)\\n    if r == 3.0 return 0 end\\n    1\\nend\\n"
    cmd: str = "cd {work} && printf {q}{body}{q} > {src} && {root}/{bin} compile {src} -o {work}/control.out >/dev/null 2>&1 && {work}/control.out >/dev/null 2>&1 && echo 1 || echo 0"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

-- BLOCKER 2, live: does the NORMATIVE grammar know the word `world` at all?
grammar(root: str, word: str): i64
    cmd: str = "cd {root} && grep -c -i -E {q}{word}{q} docs/spec/grammar.md 2>/dev/null || echo 0"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

main(): i64
    root = std.script.chomp(std.script.capture("git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null"))
    code: i64 = 0
    total: i64 = 0
    if root == ""
        print("recognition scan: FAIL — not inside a git work tree; std cannot be listed")
        code = 3
    end

    if code == 0
        total = to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture("cd {root} && git ls-files 'lib/std/*.duo' | wc -l")))
        print("recognition scan — Pass 116 §0a: no std.mem / std.fmt / std.math")
        print("  root      {root}")
        print("  modules   {total} tracked lib/std/**/*.duo")
        if total == 0
            print("recognition scan: FAIL — 0 std modules found; the scan did not run")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    -- Control 1: the pipeline itself. Every count rides this exact shell; if
    -- the control reads 0 every row below is a lie rather than a clean result.
    if code == 0
        alive: i64 = hits(root, "[a-zA-Z]")
        if alive == 0
            print("recognition scan: FAIL — the control pattern matched 0 lines of std; the grep pipeline is broken and every row below would read a confident 0")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    -- Control 2: every detector, both halves.
    if code == 0
        broken = ""
        if not sound(purepat, "  y = math.floor(x)")
            broken = "pure"
        end
        if not sound(randpat, "  r = math.random()")
            broken = "rand"
        end
        if not sound(subpat, "  f = math.fft.forward(v)")
            broken = "sub"
        end
        if not sound(nspat, "  c = std.math.clamp(x, a, b)")
            broken = "ns"
        end
        if broken != ""
            print("recognition scan: FAIL — class {broken} either missed its own example or matched ordinary Duo; a 0 from it would be a broken detector, not a clean corpus")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    -- Control 2b: the bare rows must DECLINE the dotted namespace, or `pure`
    -- and `ns` would move together and neither budget would mean anything.
    if code == 0
        if probe(purepat, "  y = std.math.floor(x)") != 0
            print("recognition scan: FAIL — the pure pattern also matches std.math.; the pure and ns budgets would double-count the same site")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    -- Control 3: the comment filter, paired.
    if code == 0
        if raw(purepat, "-- y = math.floor(x)") != 1
            print("recognition scan: FAIL — the comment control line is not matched by the pure pattern at all, so what follows would test nothing")
            code = 3
        end
    end
    if code == 0
        if probe(purepat, "-- y = math.floor(x)") != 0
            print("recognition scan: FAIL — the comment filter does not delete a commented site; every count would be inflated by prose")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    if code == 0
        pure: i64 = hits(root, purepat)
        purem: i64 = modules(root, purepat)
        pureo: i64 = outside(root, purepat)
        rand: i64 = hits(root, randpat)
        randm: i64 = modules(root, randpat)
        rando: i64 = outside(root, randpat)
        sub: i64 = hits(root, subpat)
        subm: i64 = modules(root, subpat)
        subo: i64 = outside(root, subpat)
        ns: i64 = hits(root, nspat)
        nsm: i64 = modules(root, nspat)
        nso: i64 = outside(root, nspat)

        print("")
        print("  class   sites  files  outside   repair")
        row("pure", pure, purem, pureo, "VALUE EDGE — x:abs() — no capability")
        row("rand", rand, randm, rando, "WORLD — ambient authority; U1's whole rand class")
        row("ns", ns, nsm, nso, "std.mem / std.fmt / std.math named by the ruling")
        print("")
        row("sub", sub, subm, subo, "module refs — REPORTED, NOT RATCHETED")

        print("")
        print("  outside = tracked .duo that is NOT lib/std, so the budget is never")
        print("  read as a whole-repo claim. sites are LINES: a line calling math.floor")
        print("  twice counts once, the convention capability_scan uses.")

        -- BLOCKER 1, probed live and PAIRED.
        bin: str = std.script.getenv("DUO_BIN", "zig-out/bin/duo")
        work: str = std.script.chomp(std.script.capture("mktemp -d 2>/dev/null"))
        ctl: i64 = probecontrol(root, bin, work)
        edge: i64 = probecompile(root, bin, work)
        std.script.capture("rm -rf {work}")
        print("")
        print("  BLOCKER 1 — the value-edge surface `x:abs()`, built and RUN")
        if ctl == 0
            print("    UNKNOWN — the control spelling math.abs() did not build-and-answer")
            print("    either, so the apparatus is broken (binary {bin} missing?) and NO")
            print("    verdict about x:abs() is earned from this run.")
        end
        if ctl == 1 and edge == 0
            print("    ABSENT (control builds and answers 3.0; target does not build).")
            print("    `a:abs()` lowers the receiver AWAY and emits a call to an undeclared")
            print("    `a__abs()` taking ZERO arguments — a shape that would bind to any")
            print("    same-named free function and silently drop the subject. The `pure`")
            print("    row CANNOT reach zero until this exists; converting the sites above")
            print("    today would not build. NOTE: `duo check` exits 0 on this form — only")
            print("    codegen rejects it, so `check` must never be the probe.")
        end
        if ctl == 1 and edge == 1
            print("    PRESENT — the value-edge surface builds AND answers 3.0. BLOCKER 1")
            print("    HAS LIFTED and the `pure` row is convertible. Start with the")
            print("    smallest module and verify each conversion BY VALUE.")
        end

        -- BLOCKER 2, probed live and PAIRED: `world` must be absent from the
        -- normative grammar while a control word it certainly contains is not.
        wg: i64 = grammar(root, "world")
        cg: i64 = grammar(root, "expression")
        print("")
        print("  BLOCKER 2 — worlds, the only legal capability namespace")
        if cg == 0
            print("    UNKNOWN — the control word `expression` is also absent from")
            print("    docs/spec/grammar.md, so the grep is broken, not the grammar.")
        end
        if cg > 0 and wg == 0
            print("    ABSENT (control word found {cg}x, `world` found 0x in the NORMATIVE")
            print("    grammar). There is no construct to move math.random behind. The")
            print("    `rand` row cannot reach zero, and neither can U1's rand class.")
        end
        if cg > 0 and wg > 0
            print("    PRESENT — `world` appears {wg}x in the normative grammar. Re-read it;")
            print("    BLOCKER 2 may have lifted and the rand row may be convertible.")
        end

        print("")
        print("  WITNESS. §0a also requires idiom recognition to lower UNDER WITNESS —")
        print("  `why(realization)` answers. gap[064] records `why` as undeclared. Until")
        print("  it exists, a recognized lowering has no way to prove itself, which is")
        print("  the A5 WITNESS objection to converting anything at all right now.")

        if std.script.getenv("RECOGNITION_SHOW", "") != ""
            pre: str = head(root)
            anypat = "{purepat}|{randpat}|{subpat}"
            print("")
            print("  modules by math. site count:")
            io.write(std.script.capture("{pre}{q}{anypat}{q} 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E {q}{comments}{q} | cut -d: -f1 | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -15"))
        end

        -- THE RATCHET. Measured 2026-08-08 in a detached worktree, so no
        -- session's uncommitted edits are inside the number.
        purecap: i64 = to(i64)(std.script.getenv("RECOGNITION_PURE_FLOOR", "512"))
        randcap: i64 = to(i64)(std.script.getenv("RECOGNITION_RAND_FLOOR", "51"))
        nscap: i64 = to(i64)(std.script.getenv("RECOGNITION_NS_FLOOR", "13"))
        over: i64 = 0
        if pure > purecap
            print("recognition scan: FAIL — pure sites {pure} exceeds the ratchet {purecap}")
            over += 1
        end
        if rand > randcap
            print("recognition scan: FAIL — rand sites {rand} exceeds the ratchet {randcap}")
            over += 1
        end
        if ns > nscap
            print("recognition scan: FAIL — std.mem/fmt/math sites {ns} exceeds the ratchet {nscap}")
            over += 1
        end
        if over > 0
            print("  Lower the count, or say in the commit message why the budget was wrong.")
            code = 1
        end
        if over == 0
            print("")
            print("recognition scan: PASS — pure {pure}/{purecap}, rand {rand}/{randcap}, ns {ns}/{nscap}")
            print("  PASS does not claim the ruling holds. It claims the distance did not grow.")
            print("  Both repairs are blocked on surfaces that do not exist; gaps/GAP-071.md")
            print("  holds the distance and the sequencing.")
        end
    end
    code
end
